986328125, -0.032196044921875, -0.0167694091796875, -0.033935546875, 0.04296875, 0.034454345703125, 0.01739501953125, 0.010406494140625, 0.0240325927734375, -0.0036869049072265625, 0.00612640380859375, -0.0518798828125, 0.0136871337890625, 0.004638671875, 0.01251220703125, 0.033050537109375, -0.030364990234375, 0.0016832351684570312, -0.014923095703125, -0.01209259033203125, -0.01152801513671875, 0.01739501953125, 0.0104522705078125, 0.0224151611328125, 0.0012359619140625, 0.0007481575012207031, -0.00894927978515625, 0.02166748046875, -0.01177215576171875, 0.019287109375, -0.0009546279907226562, 0.005680084228515625, 0.0171356201171875, -0.0302276611328125, 0.0026111602783203125, -0.0028400421142578125, -0.0250396728515625, -0.0197906494140625, -0.01488494873046875, 0.0192108154296875, -0.037811279296875, 0.016387939453125, -0.0022640228271484375, 0.01513671875, -0.00945281982421875, -0.0010976791381835938, -0.00524139404296875, 0.0100555419921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. This study aims to investigate whether level-0 actions and beliefs coincide, and how these are perceived by others, in two classic experimental games. To address these questions, we apply the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), which elicits both actions and beliefs from groups of participants, allowing for a more precise identification of level-0 reasoning.\n\nIn the canonical beauty contest game, where players choose numbers in an attempt to guess a fraction of the average choice, Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) found that elicited level-0 beliefs closely match observed level-0 actions, supporting the standard assumption that level-0 players choose randomly or use salient focal points. We replicate this result, finding strong alignment between level-0 beliefs and actions in our data.\n\nIn contrast, we study a variant of the 11-20 game, originally introduced by Goeree et al. (2018), in which players select integers from 11 to 20 and the highest unique number wins a prize. Our variant modifies the payoff structure by increasing the reward for choosing the highest unique number, making the incentives for strategic reasoning more pronounced. Goeree et al. (2018) observed that behavior in this game often deviates from standard level-k predictions, with players not following the iterative reasoning process assumed by the model. In our experiment, we find that elicited level-0 beliefs and observed level-0 actions diverge from each other and from the standard level-0 assumption, indicating that players may use alternative heuristics or focal points not captured by the traditional model. This divergence suggests that the behavioral patterns documented by Goeree et al. (2018) can be partly explained by heterogeneity in how level-0 reasoning is instantiated, rather than solely by higher-level strategic thinking.\n\nOur findings highlight that the correspondence between level-0 beliefs and actions is context-dependent: it holds in the beauty contest game but breaks down in the 11-20 game variant. This has important implications for level-k modeling, as it challenges the assumption that level-0 behavior is stable across games and underscores the need to account for game-specific factors in modeling bounded rationality. In summary, our study demonstrates that the identification of level-0 reasoning requires careful consideration of both beliefs and actions, and that deviations from standard assumptions can provide valuable insights into strategic behavior."}}